  "raw_text": "253\n\nTelepon\n \n(a/t:\n \nTelepong\n) (n/s)\n \n–\n \ntelephone\n \n/ teléfono\n\n(Ex/Ej:\n \nAi telepon ra witin ra winamna\n \n–\n \nI will call him on his phone\n \n/ Lo llamaré a su teléfono)\n\n(Ex/Ej:\n \nAi teleponka daswan sa\n \n–\n \nHis phone is off\n \n/\n \nSu teléfono está apagado)\n\nTem\n \n–\n \nsee/ver:\n \nTim\n\nTempel\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \ntemple\n \n/ templo\n\nSpirit lâka tempelka\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nspiritual temple\n \n/ templo espiritual\n\nTen\n \n(\nmba-\n \nfrom English / de inglés;\n \ngpm\n \nspells\n \nTin\n)\n \n–\n \nten\n \n/ diez\n\nTeps\n \n–\n \nsee/ver:\n \nTips\n\nTesalonaika\n \n(\ndb,dba\n;\n \nTesalonaiki\n-dba\n;\n \nTesalonaika Nanira\n-dbta\n;\n \nTesalonaikara\n-lrw\n)\n \n–\n \nThessalonians\n \n(Bible books) /\nTesalonicenses (libros bíblicos)\n\nTiala\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nmilk, udder, teat\n \n/ leche, ubre, tetina\n\nBîp tialka laya\n \n(a/t:\n \nBîp tyalka laya\n-\ndict-alt,my\n) (n/s)\n \n–\n \ncow milk, milk of a cow\n \n/ leche de vaca\n\nTiala dakakaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto nurse, to breastfeed\n \n/ amamantar\n\nTiala dakbaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto wean\n \n/ destetar\n\nTialka laya\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nmilk\n \n/ leche\n\n(\nNote/Nota\n: Is not used to refer to coconut milk\n, but\n \njust milk from the teat\n \n/ No se usa para referirse a la leche de coco,\n\nsino solo a la leche\n \nde la tetina\n)\n\nTialka laya subaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto milk the teat\n \n/ ordeñar la teta\n\nTiara\n \n(a/t:\n \nTyara\n) (adj,n/s)\n \n–\n \nyoung, young girl\n \n/ jovencita\n\n(Construct:\n \nTiarika\n \n(a/t:\n \nTyarka\n))\n\n(Note/Nota: Sometimes is used to refer to a\n \nyoung woman\n, but\n \nyoung girl\n \nis the more common usage / A veces se usa para\nreferirse a una\n \nmujer joven\n, pero\n \nla niña\n \nes el uso más común)\n\nMairin tiara\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nyoung girl\n \n/ jovencita\n\nSkul dimi wahma/tiara\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nyoung man/woman going to school\n \n/ el/la joven que va a la escuela\n\nTiara lupia\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nlittle girl\n \n/ niñita\n\nWahma tiara nani\n \n(a/t:\n \nWahma bara tiara nani\n) (n/s)\n \n–\n \nyoung men and young women, young people, youths,\nteenagers\n \n/ hombres y mujeres jóvenes, jóvenes, adolescentes\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nmwb-07/19\n):\n \nKangrigisan ra wahma bara tiara nani ba nahki sip sa Jehova ra prana yabaia?\n \n–\n \nHow can young men\nand women in the congregation give glory to Jehovah?\n \n/ ¿Cómo pueden los hombres y mujeres jóvenes de la\ncongregación glorificar a Jehová?)\n\n(Ex/Ej\n(\nhf23\n):\n \nWahma bara tiara nani ba aima manis pura praki aisisa\n \n–\n \nYoung people often exaggerate\n \n/\n \nLos jóvenes\n\nsuelen exagerar\n)\n\n(Ex/Ej\n(\nwqu\n-\n10/20\n):\n \nWahma tiara nani ba Jehova ra mayunaia wahbi sakisa kaka, d\nî\na blisinka nani bribia?\n \n–\n \nIf young ones\n\nchoose to worship Jehovah, what blessings will they have?\n \n/ Si los jóvenes deciden adorar a Jehová, ¿qué bendiciones\n\nrecibirán?)\n\nTibang\n \n(a/t:\n \nTibam\n,\n \nBangbang\n) (n/s)\n \n–\n \nrabbit\n \n/ conejo\n\n(Note/Nota:\n \nTibang\n \nis more common in Nicaragua than\n \nBangbang\n \n/\n \nTibang\n \nes más común en Nicaragua que\n \nBangbang\n)\n\nBangbang tara\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nhare\n \n/ liebre\n\nTibil\n \n(\na/t\n:\n \nTebel\n)\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \ntable\n \n/ mesa\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nmwb\n-\n11/20\n):\n \nNahki sip sa upla kum “pirit saura nani tibilka” wina piaia?\n \n–\n \nHow could a person “eat from the table of\n\ndemons”?\n \n/\n \n¿Cómo podría alguien estar comiendo “de la mesa de los demonios”?\n)\n\nTibil palkaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto set the table\n \n/ poner la mesa\n\nTihmia\n \n(n/s)\n \n–\n \nnight\n \n/ noche\n\n(Construct:\n \nTihmika\n)\n\n(Ex/Ej(\nmwb-12/18\n):\n \nAlmuk nani ba nit bâra sa taim, kakna bara tihmia ra hilp munisa\n \n–\n \nWhen there is a need, elders help day and\nnight\n \n/ Cuando hay una necesidad, los ancianos ayudan día y noche)\n\nNa tihmia\n \n(Alt:\n \nNaha tihmia\n)\n \n–\n \ntonight\n \n/ esta noche\n\nPamali Mayunra Tihmika\n \n–\n \nF\namily Worship Night\n \n/ Noche de\n \nA\ndoraci\nó\nn en\n \nF\namilia\n\nTihmia ikaia\n \n(v)\n \n–\n \nto get dark\n \n/ oscurecer\n \n(See also/ Ver también:\n \nTihmu ikaia\n)\n\nTihmia wâl bakriki\n \n–\n \nmidnight, middle of the night\n \n/ medianoche, medio de la noche\n\nTihmia yamni\n \n–\n \ngood night\n \n/ buenas noches\n\nTihmiaku\n \n–\n \nthrough the night\n \n/ durante la noche\n\nTihmiara\n \n(adv)\n \n–\n \nin the night, at night\n \n/ en la noche",
